==English==

{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Modern {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|epithēlium}}, from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|ἐπί||on, atop, [[epi-]]|lang=grc}} + {{term|θηλή||nipple|lang=grc}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˌɛpɪˈθiːlɪəm/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|epithelia}}

# {{context|anatomy|lang=en}} A membranous tissue composed of one or more layers of cells which forms the covering of most internal and external surfaces of the body and its organs: internally including the lining of vessels and other small cavities, and externally being the skin.
#* '''1969''', Vladimir Nabokov, ''Ada or Ardor'', Penguin 2011, p. 25:
#*: the passenger's roving eyes paused for a moment as he listened inwardly to a nether itch, which he supposed to be (correctly, thank Log) only a minor irritation of the '''epithelium'''.

====Synonyms====
* [[epithelial tissue]] 

